A||||10||William||Stuck caliper||||1||
Z||000000||William||10-23-2000||11:37 AM||||One of my front calipers is not fully releasing. I can spin the tire if I jack up the car but it's pretty tight and it groans if I rock the car. Anyone got a temporary quick fix for this? We have I meet in Jax next Saturday and I've run out of time! I spent all weekend getting the car ready and discovered this problem as I was wrapping up Sunday night. Will the wheel burst into flames if I drive it like that for a day? I'm about to upgrade the brakes anyway but I don't want to start a fire.<P>p.s. The car had sat for 3 months and I guess this has something to do with it.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||10-23-2000||11:55 AM||||I had a front caliper stick on my Fiero just after I bought it.  After my last slow down for a truck pulling off the road.  About a 1/4 mile later I was smelling smoke driving along at 50 mph.  It could catch fire under the right conditions, don't take the chance.  I got home by quickly stabing the brake a couple times once I got rolling to free the caliper enough to limp home at 45 mph.<P>On mine the caliper slides got bunged up, if that's the same problem with yours, remove the two bolts that act as the caliper slides and clean out/off the old grease.  Pack everything with that high temp "red" grease they give you with a new caliper at the parts store.  Hopefully that will be a quick fix to solve the problem, but I'd look into buying new calipers because the rubber slide seals are probably going bad and letting moisture and gunk to get into them.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">              <BR>     <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||10-23-2000||12:17 PM||||I believe that you can get the seals by themselves (try autozone) which would eliminate the need to buy a new caliper. You would just have to take the old one off, clean the slider pins, and the holes that they run thru, regrease and replace the seals. If the seals aren't availible seprately you could try getting a rebuild kit which will include them, and be definately cheaper than buying a new caliper, especially when you add the outrageous $80 core to the thing!||||reg||1
Z||000003||William||10-23-2000||12:35 PM||||How bout a really big hammer? LOL I was hoping to avoid removing the caliper as I only have about an hour total free daylight time do fix this before the meet. It's not looking good :-( Is this hard to do and do I need any special tools? I hope the chiltons book has this procedure||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||10-23-2000||02:29 PM||||You can put a C-clamp on it to "pop" it lose.  But it will need to be replaced.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||10-24-2000||08:31 AM||||It's not hard but it will take more than a few minutes if you've never had the calipers off before.<P>You'll need a torx drive (don't remember the size) to remove the two mounting bolts.  Have an old coat hanger handy to hang the caliper from to keep the strain off the brake line while it is removed from the spindle.  On the rear calipers the brake hose is held back from the spring by a clip with a hex head bolt.  I'm not sure about the fronts.  If there is one remove it to give some slack in the hose.<P>The mounting bolts are actually inside a metal sleeve that in turn slides inside the bore in the caliper.  There should be a dust boot over the bolt.  You don't have to remove the boot since there is a hole in the end for the torx drive bit.  <P>There are two o-rings inside the bore that the sleeve slides in.  The sleeves must be free to slide in and out to allow the caliper to center itself on the rotor.  Don't try to remove the bolts from inside the sleeves or you will bugger up the threads.  The sleeve is dimpled to keep the bolt from completely falling out.<P>The o-rings are available by themselves.  I got some at a local parts store (the gal at AutoZone had no idea what I was asking for).  Your o-rings are probably ok, just dirty.  Carefully remove them and clean up the bores and reinstall the o-rings with lubricant.  I used some teflon grease in a tube.  There is probably a special grease for this.  Don't use so much that it oozes out onto the rotor.<P>When you put the caliper back on it should be free to slide back and forth.  Don't forget to reattach the brake line to its support to keep it out of the spring during suspension travel.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 10-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||10-24-2000||02:24 PM||||wow, that was some good detail. unfortunatly I'm looking forward to a break job in the near future (I can feel a warped disk up front and I knowticed some un even pad wear on the back end) so I've been trying to figure out this whole slider thing. I remeber 4 bolts on the calipers, the 2 large (18-19 mm or so) bolts that hold the 2 parts of the caliper together, and 2 long bolts w/ torx heads. I'm guessing these torx heads are the 'sliders' as they slid threw the hole and only met threads for the last 1/2 inch or so. I'm confussed about greaing them tho. I remeber someone said to blue glue them, and people talking about greasing them, but I'm not quite putting 2 and 2 together (drum breaks seem so much easier all of a sudden!). ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||10-24-2000||04:21 PM||||The calipers are held to the spindle by two torx head bolts that are only threaded  for about 3/4" on the end.  They are inside of a steel sleeve that is dimpled to keep the bolt from sliding all the way out.<P>When you unthread the torx head bolts you can lift the caliper off of the rotor.  You will see the bolts and the sleeve in the bore of the caliper.  Push the sleeve/bolt out of the caliper (this could be pretty tight if they have become siezed up) but it is only held in by a pair of o-rings that are seated in grooves in the bore.  <P>I used a small socket placed against the end of the sleeve and a large pair of channel locks to push the sleeve out.<P>Once you get the sleeve/bolt out you can carefully pry the o-rings out with a bent paper clip or a pick.  Try not to damage the o-rings so you can re-use them.  Don't worry if you put a cut or gash in the o-rings since they are not actually sealing anything.  They are just there to give the sleeves something to slide through.<P>Use some WD-40 and Q-tips or a rag to clean out the gunk from the bore.  Lubricate the o-rings and slip them back into their grooves.  Clean the exterior of the sleeves and smear some lube on them and push them back through the o-rings with the end of the bolts flush with the opening.<P>Now you can slip the caliper back in place and tighten the torx head bolts back up.  The caliper should be free to slide to center itself over the rotor.<P>The lube alows the sleeves to slide inside theo-rings.  The blue glue would be to keep the torx head bolts from backing out.<P>I've never used any kind of locktite on the threads before.||||reg||1
